WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.719
In this lesson, we're looking at a problem that quietly degrades agent performance,

2
00:00:04.719 --> 00:00:09.520
tool outputs that return far more data than the agent actually needs.

3
00:00:09.520 --> 00:00:12.600
We'll cover why this bloat hurts accuracy,

4
00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:16.420
how the costs compound across multiple call workflows,

5
00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.040
and the architecture mistake that leaves a bloat in

6
00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:23.159
place even when teams think they've fixed it. Let's get into it.

7
00:00:23.159 --> 00:00:25.479
Your agent asked a weather service for

8
00:00:25.479 --> 00:00:29.799
the temperature and got back everything the service knows about the atmosphere.

9
00:00:29.799 --> 00:00:33.279
That gap is what tool output trimming closes.

10
00:00:33.279 --> 00:00:38.599
Trimming means reducing what a tool returns to only the fields the calling agent

11
00:00:38.599 --> 00:00:43.180
actually needs and you apply it at the tool wrapper, not the display layer.

12
00:00:43.180 --> 00:00:46.299
What it buys you is a better signal-to-noise ratio,

13
00:00:46.299 --> 00:00:48.720
meaning more of the context window holds

14
00:00:48.720 --> 00:00:52.439
genuinely relevant content and less of it holds filler.

15
00:00:52.439 --> 00:00:55.279
What it prevents is context window pressure,

16
00:00:55.279 --> 00:00:57.759
the state where accumulated tokens crowd

17
00:00:57.759 --> 00:01:02.520
useful content out of the regions where the model pays closest attention.

18
00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.160
Bloat lowers the ratio and raises the pressure at the same time.

19
00:01:07.160 --> 00:01:12.160
Picture a weather API that returns 80 fields, temperature, humidity,

20
00:01:12.160 --> 00:01:15.839
barometric pressure, pollen count, UV index,

21
00:01:15.839 --> 00:01:19.400
moon phase, historic averages, and dozens more.

22
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:21.239
Your agent needs three of them,

23
00:01:21.239 --> 00:01:22.519
but unless you trim,

24
00:01:22.519 --> 00:01:24.760
all 80 land in context.

25
00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:27.540
Now multiply that across an order management system,

26
00:01:27.540 --> 00:01:29.059
a user profile lookup,

27
00:01:29.059 --> 00:01:32.339
and a permissions check all in the same workflow.

28
00:01:32.339 --> 00:01:36.459
Each call deposits its full payload into the shared context.

29
00:01:36.459 --> 00:01:39.839
The agent's attention has to navigate all of it and most of

30
00:01:39.839 --> 00:01:43.580
it is completely irrelevant to what the agent is trying to accomplish.

31
00:01:43.580 --> 00:01:46.440
That gap between what's returned and what's needed

32
00:01:46.440 --> 00:01:49.739
is exactly the definition of the bloat problem.

33
00:01:49.739 --> 00:01:52.580
Here's the clearest way to see the difference.

34
00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.099
With an untrimmed output,

35
00:01:54.099 --> 00:01:56.699
all 80 fields enter the context window.

36
00:01:56.739 --> 00:01:59.500
The model reasons over timestamps, metadata,

37
00:01:59.500 --> 00:02:01.860
pagination cursors, deprecation flags,

38
00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.180
and audit stamps it doesn't need.

39
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:05.860
Signal gets diluted.

40
00:02:05.860 --> 00:02:08.779
Even when the model's logic is completely sound,

41
00:02:08.779 --> 00:02:11.220
it can land on a wrong answer because it's

42
00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:14.020
attending to noise instead of the relevant fields.

43
00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:15.300
With a trimmed output,

44
00:02:15.300 --> 00:02:18.220
only the three needed fields enter context.

45
00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:22.619
The model focuses entirely on what's relevant to the current task.

46
00:02:22.619 --> 00:02:25.500
Reasoning quality improves and the token count for

47
00:02:25.500 --> 00:02:27.820
the next request drops immediately.

48
00:02:27.820 --> 00:02:31.899
Same data source, very different outcomes for the agent.

49
00:02:31.899 --> 00:02:36.979
The bloat problem gets significantly worse when you chain tool calls together,

50
00:02:36.979 --> 00:02:40.300
which is exactly what most agentic workflows do.

51
00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:45.259
Each call deposits its unreduced payload into the shared context window.

52
00:02:45.259 --> 00:02:46.779
After five calls,

53
00:02:46.779 --> 00:02:51.899
the context holds five separate layers of noise stacked on top of each other.

54
00:02:51.899 --> 00:02:53.960
When the agent reaches step six,

55
00:02:53.960 --> 00:02:59.039
it has to reason through all that accumulated clutter from every prior step.

56
00:02:59.039 --> 00:03:02.000
The relevant signal at step one is buried under

57
00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:05.839
four more noisy payloads by the time you need it again.

58
00:03:05.839 --> 00:03:09.360
A single untrimmed call is an annoyance you can work around.

59
00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:12.720
A chain of untrimmed calls is a reliability failure

60
00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:17.039
that compounds with every additional step you add to the workflow.

61
00:03:17.039 --> 00:03:20.119
There are two cost dimensions to bloat that teams

62
00:03:20.119 --> 00:03:23.320
often overlook until they're already paying for them.

63
00:03:23.320 --> 00:03:25.279
First is token cost.

64
00:03:25.279 --> 00:03:30.679
Every extra field in context is a token you'll pay for on the next request.

65
00:03:30.679 --> 00:03:36.360
If your tool output carries 500 irrelevant tokens and you make 100 calls per session,

66
00:03:36.360 --> 00:03:39.960
that's 50,000 tokens of pure waste per session.

67
00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:44.880
Bloat you accept today becomes a recurring charge on every downstream call.

68
00:03:44.880 --> 00:03:46.440
Second is latency.

69
00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:50.839
Larger context takes more time to process and generate from.

70
00:03:50.839 --> 00:03:54.160
Even when the extra tokens don't change the final answer,

71
00:03:54.160 --> 00:03:56.440
they slow down every response.

72
00:03:56.440 --> 00:04:00.080
Trimming is one of the few optimizations that improves cost,

73
00:04:00.080 --> 00:04:03.039
speed, and accuracy simultaneously.

74
00:04:03.039 --> 00:04:07.800
Research on transformer attention has shown that models attend most strongly to

75
00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:12.080
content positioned near the start and end of the context window.

76
00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:16.519
Content buried in the middle receives measurably weaker attention.

77
00:04:16.519 --> 00:04:19.920
This phenomenon is called the lost in the middle effect,

78
00:04:20.079 --> 00:04:24.160
and it has direct practical consequences for agent design.

79
00:04:24.160 --> 00:04:30.760
When verbose tool outputs push key facts toward the center of a long context, accuracy drops.

80
00:04:30.760 --> 00:04:35.079
It's not because the model reasoned badly or has a logic problem.

81
00:04:35.079 --> 00:04:38.760
It's because the critical signal ended up in a low attention zone,

82
00:04:38.760 --> 00:04:41.839
where the model simply doesn't focus as strongly.

83
00:04:41.839 --> 00:04:45.640
Trimming keeps your relevant signal in high attention positions

84
00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.079
where it will actually be weighted appropriately by the model.

85
00:04:50.279 --> 00:04:54.399
One of the most common mistakes I see in production agent architectures

86
00:04:54.399 --> 00:04:58.160
is trimming at the display layer instead of the model layer.

87
00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.440
Teams build a clean dashboard that shows only the three relevant fields

88
00:05:02.440 --> 00:05:04.000
to human reviewers.

89
00:05:04.000 --> 00:05:07.839
The interface looks polished and the team feels like they've solved the bloat

90
00:05:07.839 --> 00:05:12.359
problem, but the model is still receiving the full 80 field payload

91
00:05:12.359 --> 00:05:16.000
in its context window every single call.

92
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.959
The display trim hides the noise from humans,

93
00:05:18.959 --> 00:05:21.000
but it does nothing for the model.

94
00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:23.959
The agent is still reasoning over all that filler.

95
00:05:23.959 --> 00:05:28.040
The fix belongs at the tool wrapper, where field level filtering

96
00:05:28.040 --> 00:05:32.799
declares a fixed allow list of fields to keep and drops everything else

97
00:05:32.799 --> 00:05:35.839
before the output ever enters context.

98
00:05:35.839 --> 00:05:38.160
Downstream display can be handled separately.

99
00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:41.799
Model side trim comes first.

100
00:05:41.799 --> 00:05:44.519
Verbose tool outputs hurt agent performance

101
00:05:44.519 --> 00:05:49.239
in ways that are easy to miss until the workflow is already in production.

102
00:05:49.239 --> 00:05:52.119
This bloat compounds across calls.

103
00:05:52.119 --> 00:05:56.600
The token and latency costs accumulate on every downstream request,

104
00:05:56.600 --> 00:06:00.440
and accuracy erodes not because the model reasons badly,

105
00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:05.279
but because the signal ended up buried where the model reads last.

106
00:06:05.279 --> 00:06:09.760
Trim at the wrapper before the output ever reaches the model.

107
00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:11.399
That closes out this lesson.

108
00:06:11.399 --> 00:06:13.799
I'll see you in the next one.

